      "problem": "Find the value of $\\frac{2}{3}-1\\,\\frac{1}{3}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we write $1\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{(1\\times 3)+1}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nSince both fractions have the same denominators, we can combine them into one fraction, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}=\\frac{2-4}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we work out $2-4$ using a number line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216627/assets/fd7fc3fedd2dbf8c90592bbedfdc15181eae63e4d2cbd71a4c036b2d75e8921c.png)\n\nSo, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{2-4}{3}=\\frac{-2}{3}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$",
